WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.820 --> 00:00:15.740
Hello everyone and welcome to a special

00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:16.680
Win-Win episode.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.000
An episode where you get to see me cry

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:20.200
over a chicken dumpling.

00:00:20.860 --> 00:00:22.760
Because I recently visited Uma Valeti,

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.880
the CEO and founder of Upside Foods.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.700
Now Upside are one of a growing number

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:29.420
of companies developing what is known

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:31.380
as cultivated meat.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:33.860
Meat that is 100 % animal.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:36.540
But instead of being grown on the

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:38.460
skeleton of an animal in a farm, that

00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:40.440
meat is grown in a bioreactor.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.780
Which I appreciate sounds rather sci-fi.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:45.400
But as someone who is a huge meat

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.600
lover, and also a huge animal lover,

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.560
this technology is a real win-win in my book.

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.160
Because let's face it, the modern meat

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:56.340
industry sucks, especially factory

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.320
farms, not only in how they treat their

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.580
animals, but also what they're doing to

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.620
our environment, and even to our own

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.560
health with things like antibiotic resistance.

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.200
So for me at least, this technology

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.900
provides an elegant solution to the

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.620
cognitive dissonance that I often have

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.100
around my own carnivorousness.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.760
This episode is a look behind the

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:16.680
scenes of what it's taken to bring this

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:18.320
technology to a point where it is

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:19.660
almost ready for market.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:22.560
And quick disclaimer, I am an investor

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.340
in Upside Foods and wasn't paid or even

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:26.120
asked make this video.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.260
I just really, really believe in this technology.

00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:31.920
So I'm happily shilling for it, for

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:32.680
reasons you'll hear.

00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:34.820
But please do keep that in mind as you

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:35.420
watch, obviously.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.020
That out of the way, let's dig in.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.220
So Uma, what are we going to be trying today?

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.880
We're going to try a number of things

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:55.860
that you can do with chicken.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:01:56.900
He's doing dumplings.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.600
He's going to make a breakfast sausage,

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:01.240
complete whole cut chicken breast, that

00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:02.720
we do our own version of.

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.740
And then we're going to go into fried chicken.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:06.660
We wanted to pick something very familiar.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:09.220
Chicken is the most familiar meat.

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.060
People love eating chicken in many

00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.000
forms and we're like, let's not

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.260
compromise that experience.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.220
People should recognize that when it

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:19.780
hits their taste buds, oh yeah, I know that.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:20.300
Yes.

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.500
So as you can imagine, I was pretty

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.180
excited to try this stuff.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:26.740
But first, let's hear how Uma went from

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:29.620
being a cardiologist to a cultivated

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:30.340
meat grower.

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:35.090
When I was 12 I went to a friend's

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:36.530
birthday party, our neighbor.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:38.230
We were in the front of the house

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.550
having a lot of fun and then I just

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:43.750
walked to the back of the house and in

00:02:43.750 --> 00:02:45.830
the back of the house was where they

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:47.530
were slaughtering the animals to feed

00:02:47.530 --> 00:02:48.330
the people in the front.

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:50.390
I think that was the first time ever I

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:55.130
came face to face with the duality of

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:55.810
meat production.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:57.610
I didn't do anything about it then.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:02:58.750
I kept eating meat.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:00.330
I loved eating meat and then I went

00:03:00.330 --> 00:03:00.870
medical school.

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:03.190
So we had this group of 50, 60 kids

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:06.170
that were in one medical school.

00:03:06.610 --> 00:03:09.170
I volunteered to run the cafeteria and

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:10.550
during that time I used to go with the

00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:12.270
chefs and the sous-chefs to the market

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.370
and buy all of the produce and all of

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:14.710
the stuff.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:16.550
One day we went to actually the meat,

00:03:16.970 --> 00:03:20.450
the slaughterhouse and I saw for the

00:03:20.450 --> 00:03:24.070
first time intensified, intense animal

00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:25.330
production operations chickens.

00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:27.790
And that was like a moment I can't forget.

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:30.410
I'm like, on a chain, there were 60

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:32.610
chickens every second being

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.150
slaughtered, moved to the next thing,

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:36.330
slaughtered, moved to the next thing.

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:38.910
I did not know how to react.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:40.270
But I think those two moments, the

00:03:40.270 --> 00:03:41.730
birthday, death day when I was 12 years

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.530
old, and this was when I was 18 years old.

00:03:44.470 --> 00:03:47.370
I'm like, okay, I love eating meat, but

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:48.570
I'm going to stop eating meat.

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:49.970
And that's what, that's the choice I

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:50.610
made for myself.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.010
I didn't tell anyone.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:54.510
And then I came to the US to do

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:55.950
cardiology at the Mayo Clinic, I was

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:58.070
fortunate to be able to get there.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:00.730
And as I was training to be a

00:04:00.730 --> 00:04:01.790
cardiologist, international

00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:03.950
cardiologist, we also started working

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:04.770
on stem cells.

00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:08.530
And stem cells are these incredibly

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:11.390
versatile cells in every person's body,

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:14.110
are also animals bodies where these

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:15.090
cells can do anything.

00:04:15.670 --> 00:04:17.890
They can become muscle, they can become

00:04:17.890 --> 00:04:19.490
connective tissue, they can become

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:21.350
bone, they can become brain cells.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:23.930
cells, and what we were doing were

00:04:23.930 --> 00:04:27.350
taking those cells and injecting them

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:28.690
into patients' hearts when they had a

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:29.990
heart attack or a cardiac arrest.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:31.630
As I started thinking about it, I'm

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:33.530
like, could we actually do this with

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:35.930
animals and take cells from an animal

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:36.590
and grow meat?

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:38.510
And that was somewhere in the range of,

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:41.670
I think, 2005 or so, that idea came

00:04:41.670 --> 00:04:44.110
into my head and I could not get it out.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:45.490
I just could not get it.

00:04:45.550 --> 00:04:47.150
I tried really hard because there was

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:48.430
lots of things going on in cardiology,

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:50.010
but every Every morning I wake up and

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:51.190
that thought comes back to my mind.

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:53.930
So I started talking about that with my

00:04:53.930 --> 00:04:57.450
friends, my family, and finally people,

00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:59.230
they got fed up with me talking about

00:04:59.230 --> 00:05:00.210
it and not doing anything.

00:05:00.490 --> 00:05:02.130
And they said, why are you not doing it?

00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:05.450
So that became a call to action.

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:09.290
But how does it work?

00:05:09.550 --> 00:05:11.810
Like how can you begin to grow meat

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:13.470
without its host animal?

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:16.490
So the process starts with us taking

00:05:16.490 --> 00:05:18.870
cells from either an adult animal,

00:05:19.010 --> 00:05:20.190
young animal, or an egg.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:22.870
It's a small biopsy, and a biopsy

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.870
usually gets you one or two drops worth

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:27.430
of cells from any part of the animal.

00:05:27.890 --> 00:05:29.930
And once we do that, we take those

00:05:29.930 --> 00:05:31.390
cells and we grow them outside the

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:34.330
animal with nutrients and identify the

00:05:34.330 --> 00:05:36.530
animal cells that do a fantastic job of

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:38.290
growing and retaining the flavor and

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:38.690
the textures.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:40.730
They are the ones that graduate to

00:05:40.730 --> 00:05:42.850
become a commercial breed at Upside.

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:44.830
Once they do that, we don't have to go

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:45.510
back to the animal.

00:05:46.130 --> 00:05:47.450
So for instance, these chickens

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:50.410
products you're eating today, we got

00:05:50.410 --> 00:05:53.770
them from the source about four or five

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:56.890
years ago from a chicken, and we

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:57.630
haven't had to go back.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:05:59.530
We can keep producing with that for the

00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:00.230
next 30 years.

00:06:00.490 --> 00:06:03.590
The DNA is identical to the sort of old

00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:04.570
school style of chicken.

00:06:04.730 --> 00:06:06.990
Yeah, I mean, the DNA here is chicken DNA.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:09.330
Like, if you just go and do, put it

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:12.610
through any DNA reading and ask it to

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:14.590
read what species is this, it'll say

00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:15.350
gallus gallus.

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:18.490
So the DNA question is probably the

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:20.330
biggest misunderstanding I keep hearing

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:21.430
about cultivated meat.

00:06:22.070 --> 00:06:24.150
People understandably assume that it

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:26.550
must be genetically modified in some

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:27.930
way in order to grow.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:29.230
But it's not.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:31.210
It's genetically identical to whatever

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:32.850
farmed animal the cell samples

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:33.830
originally come from.

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:35.930
So it's not a GMO food.

00:06:36.230 --> 00:06:38.810
What is different though is how much

00:06:38.810 --> 00:06:41.350
time and energy and space it takes to

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:43.070
grow the meat, especially when you

00:06:43.070 --> 00:06:45.330
compare it pound for pound to current methods.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:48.430
Any meat takes about two weeks, maybe

00:06:48.430 --> 00:06:49.770
it takes three weeks, maybe it takes

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:51.130
four weeks if you're trying to add some

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:52.590
exquisite features into it.

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:55.010
But let's say it averages out at two

00:06:55.010 --> 00:06:57.030
weeks or let's push it to three weeks.

00:06:57.350 --> 00:07:00.030
When you compare that with a cow, which

00:07:00.030 --> 00:07:02.050
is two years, a pig, which is nine

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:03.750
months, and and the chicken, which is

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:04.270
two months.

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:06.130
And the lamb is about six months.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:10.530
It's just a much shorter process, 4X to

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:12.330
almost 50X.

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:14.170
And in terms of energy efficiency,

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:18.130
again, I don't know if you can make a

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:19.850
comparison, but how much energy it

00:07:19.850 --> 00:07:24.970
takes to get a kilogram of steak versus

00:07:24.970 --> 00:07:29.590
a kilogram of steak grown in a lab, how

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:31.170
much more energy is that?

00:07:31.170 --> 00:07:31.210
Yes.

00:07:31.430 --> 00:07:33.430
So we have our own work that we've done

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:36.110
and started to say, at scale, when we

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:38.490
project out our production methods, the

00:07:38.490 --> 00:07:40.490
efficiency and the time it takes for us

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:42.690
to grow the meat and compare with the

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:44.370
literature out there, the majority of

00:07:44.370 --> 00:07:47.710
it is saying unequivocally that when we

00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:49.830
get to scale of tens of millions of

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:50.890
pounds or hundreds of millions of

00:07:50.890 --> 00:07:54.770
pounds, the land use goes down to about

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:58.310
90 % less than what we need for

00:07:58.310 --> 00:07:59.130
conventional meat.

00:07:59.130 --> 00:08:01.610
meat, the water use goes down by about

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:03.870
90 plus percent to what it requires to

00:08:03.870 --> 00:08:06.170
feed an animal before it's slaughtered.

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:09.790
And the greenhouse gas emissions also

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:11.090
go down by about 90 percent.

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:12.930
So those three things, greenhouse gas

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:15.030
emissions, land use, water use, go down

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:15.810
about 90 percent.

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:18.210
We have 70 billion animals right now

00:08:18.210 --> 00:08:20.490
drinking a third of all the fresh water

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:22.450
that we have in the world, using a

00:08:22.450 --> 00:08:24.830
third of all the arable land to make

00:08:24.830 --> 00:08:25.890
the feed for them.

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:28.050
and if the demand for meat is doubling,

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:29.570
simple math.

00:08:29.990 --> 00:08:31.290
That means animals will be drinking

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:32.830
two-thirds of all the fresh water 30

00:08:32.830 --> 00:08:34.590
years from now that's on Earth, and

00:08:34.590 --> 00:08:36.730
two-thirds of all the agricultural land

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:38.190
is going to be used to grow crops for

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:39.550
feeding animals to feed humans.

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:41.050
That math won't work out.

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:42.510
And the thing is, he is right.

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:44.310
As more and more poor countries

00:08:44.310 --> 00:08:46.430
modernize, trends show that their

00:08:46.430 --> 00:08:48.230
demand for meat will significantly

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:50.270
increase, which is going to make our

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:52.090
current, very inefficient farming

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:54.870
system even more unsustainable long-term.

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:57.030
A statistic that really blows my mind

00:08:57.030 --> 00:09:00.270
is just how much the distribution of

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:02.490
biology on Earth has changed since the

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:03.630
agricultural revolution.

00:09:04.050 --> 00:09:06.330
Before humans started farming, we only

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:08.690
made up a tiny sliver of all the

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:09.770
biomass on Earth.

00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:13.930
Now we and our livestock represent 98 %

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:15.670
of all biomass.

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:18.750
That's more than a 10,000x increase,

00:09:19.250 --> 00:09:20.530
much of which has only happened in the

00:09:20.530 --> 00:09:21.630
last couple hundred years.

00:09:22.090 --> 00:09:24.690
So such a rapid swing is obviously

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:26.930
going to create a huge shock on any

00:09:26.930 --> 00:09:29.290
ecosystem, but ultimately I can throw

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:31.310
stats around all day long and none of

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:33.430
this matters if this meat doesn't

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:35.590
actually taste good, or at least taste

00:09:35.590 --> 00:09:37.090
as good as what you can buy in a supermarket.

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:41.350
So here is my rather emotional taste test.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:43.050
Does the product feel different in any

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:43.750
way to cook with?

00:09:44.170 --> 00:09:46.170
No, we've kind of put it through its

00:09:46.170 --> 00:09:47.590
paces, you know, we've kind of tried

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:48.850
all sorts of stuff.

00:09:49.010 --> 00:09:51.510
We've fried it, we've brined it, we've

00:09:51.510 --> 00:09:53.690
smoked it, we've made sausages, we've

00:09:53.690 --> 00:09:54.770
made kind of everything that you could

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:55.430
kind of imagine.

00:09:55.790 --> 00:09:57.130
And yeah, it really acts in the same

00:09:57.130 --> 00:09:58.050
way as you would expect chicken.

00:09:58.550 --> 00:10:00.290
So this is the potsticker, so like Uma

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:02.210
said, this is cultivated chicken.

00:10:02.350 --> 00:10:04.810
It has some ginger, scallion, kind of

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:06.170
traditional seasonings in there.

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:07.650
Have you ever tasted cultivated meat,

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:07.990
by the way?

00:10:08.090 --> 00:10:10.270
I have never tasted cultivated meat before.

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:13.070
I have been waiting years to do this.

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:16.530
As someone who like I love meats but I

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:19.750
love animals and I love earth this is

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:21.770
like this is the ultimate win-win

00:10:21.770 --> 00:10:24.810
technology I'm quite actually

00:10:43.000 --> 00:10:50.740
emotionally well we're exactly the same

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:58.780
it's so good it's I mean it's it's I

00:10:58.780 --> 00:11:00.180
don't think I would there's no way I

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:01.860
could tell the difference that's

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:03.800
amazing thank you for saying that this

00:11:03.800 --> 00:11:05.300
is the reaction we get every single

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:06.720
time people come in and taste it.

00:11:07.100 --> 00:11:08.420
It just feels really good.

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:09.880
Wow.

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:12.480
We have a lot more products.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:13.760
I'm so impressed.

00:11:14.080 --> 00:11:15.460
Has anyone ever cried before when

00:11:15.460 --> 00:11:15.880
they've eaten it?

00:11:16.540 --> 00:11:17.100
A few have.

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:18.300
A few have?

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:19.000
Okay, good.

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:19.960
So I'm not the only winner.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:24.320
It's actually, I can't believe it's real.

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:26.020
I can't believe it's real.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:28.980
So yes, I can definitively say it

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:31.020
passed the taste test, at least to me.

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:33.020
But of course, the question you're

00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:35.260
probably all wondering is, what are the

00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:37.680
health and safety the implications of

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:38.300
this technology.

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:40.400
I mean, after all, we've evolved to eat

00:11:40.400 --> 00:11:43.140
meat conventionally for thousands of years.

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:45.560
And this is as far away from nature as

00:11:45.560 --> 00:11:46.520
you can get, really.

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:48.140
So how can these companies ensure that

00:11:48.140 --> 00:11:50.640
it lives up to safety and nutritional standards?

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:52.460
Presumably, you don't actually have

00:11:52.460 --> 00:11:54.180
any, you can't have any long-term

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:56.780
studies, right, on the effect of humans

00:11:56.780 --> 00:11:59.480
eating cultivated meat because it's so new.

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:01.000
You know, the first person ate, what,

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:01.620
in 2013?

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:06.080
Mark Post Burger, in 2013 August, about

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:08.080
10 years ago, is the first time that

00:12:08.080 --> 00:12:09.980
was eaten publicly, televised.

00:12:10.560 --> 00:12:11.700
But there have been people who have

00:12:11.700 --> 00:12:13.640
been tasting it before that, and at

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:14.740
various small events.

00:12:15.140 --> 00:12:16.460
But you're right, it all has been

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:19.240
happening in the last 10-15 years.

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:22.480
Are there any studies planned to sort

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:25.120
of measure the effects on people who

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:26.700
have been eating it consistently and so on?

00:12:26.700 --> 00:12:27.740
because I imagine there's going to be a

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:30.120
lot of people who, you know, aren't

00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:32.080
willing to try it until there is some

00:12:32.080 --> 00:12:33.160
sort of long-term study.

00:12:33.300 --> 00:12:34.020
I think there will be.

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:36.800
You know, I get calls from people that

00:12:36.800 --> 00:12:38.440
are very interested in doing studies

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:40.500
and people that have scientifically

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:42.000
rigorous things that want to do

00:12:42.000 --> 00:12:42.780
long-term studies.

00:12:42.860 --> 00:12:43.800
And I said, those should happen.

00:12:44.320 --> 00:12:46.200
They will happen as they should happen

00:12:46.200 --> 00:12:47.820
in a very well-designed fashion.

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:50.000
It'll take time to make all of these

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:52.280
studies happen, but it'll come back to

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:53.780
the challenge, which is, what do you

00:12:53.780 --> 00:12:54.360
compare it with?

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:55.240
Where is the gold standard?

00:12:55.240 --> 00:12:56.280
What do you accept?

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:59.380
If you accept a third of us could

00:12:59.380 --> 00:13:01.260
develop cancers from the food that we

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:04.020
eat, we're already at that level right now.

00:13:04.580 --> 00:13:07.920
So in scientific design of a proper

00:13:07.920 --> 00:13:10.060
study that can be interpreted well, we

00:13:10.060 --> 00:13:11.380
really have to keep those things in

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:13.280
mind and start questioning that because

00:13:13.280 --> 00:13:15.180
that's also changed over time.

00:13:15.780 --> 00:13:17.080
There's so many of these caveats that

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:18.420
we need to think about designing studies.

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:20.000
And what I would say is, let's step back.

00:13:20.280 --> 00:13:22.660
Let's step back and say, are there any

00:13:22.660 --> 00:13:25.500
studies that just say, I'm going to put

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:27.660
people in eating conventional meat-only

00:13:27.660 --> 00:13:30.300
category and people that don't eat any

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:31.540
meat at all in their diet category?

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:33.720
Do we actually have really strong

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:35.640
evidence to say one group is at a

00:13:35.640 --> 00:13:36.960
higher risk than the other group?

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:38.940
Now, if that study has not been done,

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:40.760
then in that case, if you do a

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:43.140
meta-study on top of it without a

00:13:43.140 --> 00:13:45.200
proper foundation, it becomes very

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:46.120
challenging to interpret.

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:48.300
So the way we approach it is the following.

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:50.100
We're like, let's make sure that we

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:52.120
know everything possible about the

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:54.300
cell, we know everything possible about

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:55.760
the nutrition that is going into these

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:58.140
cells, and we know the growing

00:13:58.140 --> 00:14:00.380
conditions, and everything that we do

00:14:00.380 --> 00:14:01.120
is trackable.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:03.560
How can you ensure that the meat

00:14:03.560 --> 00:14:07.100
products are as healthy for you as, you

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:08.180
know, say a chicken breast I would go

00:14:08.180 --> 00:14:11.140
buy, you know, from a conventional farm?

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:13.360
Or, you know, perhaps more accurately,

00:14:13.500 --> 00:14:14.980
let's compare it to like a factory farm.

00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:17.660
We can compare to free range as well,

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:18.760
but maybe in a more factory farms,

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:20.880
which is where, you know, 90 % of meat

00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:22.000
is coming from nowadays, right?

00:14:22.120 --> 00:14:24.660
So we think about putting products that

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:27.020
are nutritionally rich and part of a

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:27.960
well-balanced diet.

00:14:27.960 --> 00:14:31.140
Conventional meat, as we know it, and

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:33.240
processed meats, as we know it, have

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:34.980
been associated with certain diseases,

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:37.780
whether it's cardiovascular disease or

00:14:37.780 --> 00:14:41.460
cancers or chronic diseases or diseases

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:43.100
that could lead to inflammation.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:45.240
Now, when I step back as a physician

00:14:45.240 --> 00:14:47.860
and think about what are we trying to

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:50.600
really do here, I think the opportunity

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:52.980
with cultivated meat to start making

00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:55.160
meat better is significantly higher

00:14:55.160 --> 00:14:56.540
than you can ever imagine with

00:14:56.540 --> 00:14:57.240
conventional meat.

00:14:57.640 --> 00:14:58.740
And a couple of examples.

00:14:59.260 --> 00:15:03.780
If we know how to select cells that are

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:06.780
capable of giving us the right omega-3,

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:09.780
omega-6 ratios, Suddenly it's like,

00:15:09.920 --> 00:15:12.180
okay, can I make beef with the health

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:14.040
benefits for the heart, like a salmon?

00:15:14.620 --> 00:15:16.580
Those possibilities open up.

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:19.560
And that's when my eyes light up, our

00:15:19.560 --> 00:15:21.600
team's eyes light up, and say, yes, we

00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:22.000
see that.

00:15:22.060 --> 00:15:23.220
We can start thinking about touching

00:15:23.220 --> 00:15:23.680
that future.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:26.720
Do you need to use, because I know in

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:28.200
conventional farming, and especially in

00:15:28.200 --> 00:15:30.040
factory farms, the amount of antibiotic

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:33.040
usage is, I couldn't find the exact

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:34.300
numbers on what percentage of

00:15:34.300 --> 00:15:35.920
antibiotics worldwide are actually

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:37.780
given to farm animals, but it's a very

00:15:37.780 --> 00:15:39.980
significant amount, which is obviously

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:41.740
contributing to antibiotic resistance,

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:44.960
other sort of growing threats.

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:48.700
And people are quite understandably

00:15:48.700 --> 00:15:50.060
concerned about the idea that they're

00:15:50.060 --> 00:15:52.820
ingesting meat from animals that have

00:15:52.820 --> 00:15:54.560
been consuming so many antibiotics.

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:57.020
Do you have to use them in your systems?

00:15:57.540 --> 00:15:58.360
Answer is no.

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:01.480
All the products you eat, they never

00:16:01.480 --> 00:16:02.200
say antibiotics.

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:05.200
And that's what's going to be another

00:16:05.200 --> 00:16:07.080
distinct advantage cultivated meat, right?

00:16:07.160 --> 00:16:08.720
As we start scaling our production

00:16:08.720 --> 00:16:10.200
techniques and keep going to scale,

00:16:10.440 --> 00:16:12.100
people are going to see that.

00:16:13.240 --> 00:16:16.480
And 60 to 80 % of all the antibiotics

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:19.880
made in the United States are made for

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:21.800
animals, not for humans.

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:23.380
That's a very large percentage.

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:25.820
So what's happening to all of that antibiotics?

00:16:26.560 --> 00:16:28.080
The animal is eating and then it's

00:16:28.080 --> 00:16:30.460
secreting it or excreting it, and it

00:16:30.460 --> 00:16:32.800
gets into the runoff, and then we get

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:33.600
exposed to that.

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:36.900
And there's a lot of worry about

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:37.500
antibiotic resistance.

00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:39.360
And we've seen lots of antibiotic

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:41.960
resistance organisms with hundreds of

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:43.220
thousands of people being affected

00:16:43.220 --> 00:16:45.620
every year and tens of thousands dying

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:46.080
from it.

00:16:46.380 --> 00:16:48.160
So that's one distinct advantage.

00:16:49.060 --> 00:16:51.100
The second one is pandemic risk.

00:16:51.360 --> 00:16:53.640
I worry about that even more because

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:56.160
antibiotic resistance, major problem,

00:16:56.360 --> 00:16:58.440
can affect pockets of people and it

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:01.040
could be a It's a slow-rolling disaster.

00:17:01.900 --> 00:17:03.520
When you suddenly think about

00:17:03.520 --> 00:17:06.320
pandemics, the biggest risk that we are

00:17:06.320 --> 00:17:08.380
facing and looking at every day in the

00:17:08.380 --> 00:17:11.300
eye is, if you are confining 70 billion

00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:14.660
animals every year in tiny, tiny areas,

00:17:15.220 --> 00:17:17.440
and those animals are so close to each

00:17:17.440 --> 00:17:19.200
other, in the middle of breeding,

00:17:20.120 --> 00:17:22.660
infectious agents circulating, and

00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:24.600
excreting their excrement all being in

00:17:24.600 --> 00:17:25.160
one place.

00:17:25.460 --> 00:17:26.220
It's like a Petri dish.

00:17:26.420 --> 00:17:28.380
It's the literal petri dish.

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:30.500
Talk about an entire lab-grown

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:31.920
experiment in the world that we do

00:17:31.920 --> 00:17:35.000
every single second on a massive,

00:17:35.140 --> 00:17:35.700
massive scale.

00:17:35.780 --> 00:17:37.960
That is the lab-grown experiment that

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:39.060
I'm very scared of.

00:17:39.520 --> 00:17:41.860
We've lived that through the pandemic,

00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:44.760
of what it can cause to our lives and

00:17:44.760 --> 00:17:45.400
do to our lives.

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:50.160
I lost my dad from COVID, and he's a veterinarian.

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:52.400
He and we used to talk about this

00:17:52.400 --> 00:17:54.980
pandemic risk, always, zoonotic

00:17:54.980 --> 00:17:57.100
diseases spreading from an animal,

00:17:57.960 --> 00:18:00.280
either a wild animal or a domesticated

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:02.560
animal jumping into humans, always

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:03.260
talked about it.

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:06.240
It just is really sad that I lost my

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:06.880
dad to it.

00:18:07.660 --> 00:18:09.880
But when these things start happening

00:18:09.880 --> 00:18:12.680
to people, we've got to be able to say,

00:18:12.880 --> 00:18:14.420
let's open our eyes wide.

00:18:15.020 --> 00:18:17.040
Let's look at opportunities where we

00:18:17.040 --> 00:18:18.400
can decrease that risk.

00:18:19.100 --> 00:18:21.900
I'd love to get into the sort of more

00:18:21.900 --> 00:18:23.180
economic side of it.

00:18:23.180 --> 00:18:24.300
Yes, let's do it.

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:31.000
So, initially, the production of, I

00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:33.020
think, a pound of beef through

00:18:33.020 --> 00:18:35.720
cultivated meat was $18,000 a pound.

00:18:36.260 --> 00:18:38.040
Chicken was $9,000 a pound.

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:42.060
Where is the price down to

00:18:42.060 --> 00:18:43.200
approximately now?

00:18:43.200 --> 00:18:46.140
And how do you plan to scale it so that

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:48.760
it becomes commercially competitive to

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:49.480
conventional meat?

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:51.960
This is a fantastic question.

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:54.320
It comes every single day for us.

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:57.200
And our answer is there's three things

00:18:57.200 --> 00:18:58.000
we are focusing on.

00:18:58.000 --> 00:18:59.920
People have to fall in love with the product.

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:02.260
People have to be able to afford it.

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:06.040
And then people have to be able to see

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:07.000
how it's made.

00:19:07.300 --> 00:19:08.580
Those are a must-have.

00:19:08.980 --> 00:19:11.380
How do we take care of the first taste thing?

00:19:11.620 --> 00:19:13.520
That's the first magical thing for

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:14.100
people to see.

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:17.340
They have to come into a kitchen, see

00:19:17.340 --> 00:19:18.860
it being cooked, and taste, right?

00:19:19.700 --> 00:19:21.200
We're never going to pass the sniff

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:23.060
test if we don't get across that.

00:19:23.120 --> 00:19:24.640
So that was the laser focus for the

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:26.700
last, for the first three, four years.

00:19:27.120 --> 00:19:29.020
The focus now is cost.

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:31.140
We're like, we want to be able to put

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:31.960
this product on the market.

00:19:32.100 --> 00:19:33.480
It's going to be expensive to make it

00:19:33.480 --> 00:19:36.260
in the early days, but we do want to be

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:38.960
able to price it for a consumer in the

00:19:38.960 --> 00:19:41.500
range of a premium meat product that

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:42.820
they're going to be able to go and buy

00:19:42.820 --> 00:19:43.600
at a restaurant.

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:44.820
If they're buying a chicken sandwich,

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:46.260
if they're paying $15 for a chicken

00:19:46.260 --> 00:19:48.380
sandwich now, they're like, we want to

00:19:48.380 --> 00:19:50.520
be able to capture a slight premium on

00:19:50.520 --> 00:19:52.420
that because it helps us continue to do

00:19:52.420 --> 00:19:53.300
the research and innovation.

00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:56.080
So what we're thinking is price it

00:19:56.080 --> 00:19:59.480
above organic in the range of 30 % or so

00:19:59.480 --> 00:20:00.080
above organic.

00:20:00.340 --> 00:20:03.000
That we think a lot of people can start

00:20:03.000 --> 00:20:05.280
affording to pay right now, enough for

00:20:05.280 --> 00:20:06.700
us to be able to keep building the

00:20:06.700 --> 00:20:08.780
business and start saying, we'll get

00:20:08.780 --> 00:20:11.140
closer and closer to conventional meat

00:20:11.140 --> 00:20:14.300
price parity, which will be another 50 %

00:20:14.300 --> 00:20:16.220
lower than what an organic chicken

00:20:16.220 --> 00:20:16.960
costs right now.

00:20:16.960 --> 00:20:19.180
Those are what our North Stars are, and

00:20:19.180 --> 00:20:20.400
our first products that we'll put on

00:20:20.400 --> 00:20:22.300
the market will be in that range.

00:20:22.980 --> 00:20:25.440
The cost of manufacturing it is higher

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:27.280
right now because we're doing it in

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:30.900
small-scale, a lot more bespoke craft

00:20:30.900 --> 00:20:33.420
type of manufacturing, but when we are

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:35.600
building the next commercial facility

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:37.260
based on what we are sitting in right now.

00:20:37.640 --> 00:20:40.000
That is focused on bringing the cost of

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:41.980
chicken to $10 a pound.

00:20:42.940 --> 00:20:44.260
That's the goal we've got.

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:47.740
The next one is to get it to $5 a pound.

00:20:48.020 --> 00:20:49.100
I think that is kind of where it's

00:20:49.100 --> 00:20:50.760
going if we use today's prices.

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:52.400
What about the role of subsidies?

00:20:52.680 --> 00:20:54.200
Because I think what a lot of people

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:55.680
don't realize is, you know, a lot of

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:57.120
the meat and food that we eat is

00:20:57.120 --> 00:20:59.140
already very subsidized, right?

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:02.060
Is that the case with meat?

00:21:02.060 --> 00:21:04.460
And do you think it could be the case

00:21:04.460 --> 00:21:05.420
with cultivated meat too?

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:07.780
The conventional industry has taken

00:21:07.780 --> 00:21:10.180
advantage of enormous amounts of

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:13.740
subsidies, incentives, loan programs,

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:17.380
tax benefits to get to a place of

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:19.440
efficiency that they're at right now.

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:19.760
Right.

00:21:20.200 --> 00:21:22.960
That took several decades for them to

00:21:22.960 --> 00:21:23.420
get there.

00:21:23.780 --> 00:21:25.640
And there has been an incredible amount

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:28.360
of taxpayer support private, and

00:21:28.360 --> 00:21:29.940
public-private partnerships.

00:21:30.760 --> 00:21:33.180
And I think if we have to be realistic

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:35.280
and say a transformative change like

00:21:35.280 --> 00:21:39.400
this should be funded similar to that,

00:21:39.460 --> 00:21:40.460
that's a level playing field.

00:21:40.560 --> 00:21:43.740
And we will have to be eligible for all

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:45.340
of these same benefits that

00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:46.880
conventional meat industry has been

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:49.680
able to avail for cultivated meat to go

00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:50.300
to the next level.

00:21:50.520 --> 00:21:52.380
And there's great examples of that that

00:21:52.380 --> 00:21:53.120
we've been living through.

00:21:53.520 --> 00:21:54.920
Think about the energy transformation

00:21:54.920 --> 00:21:55.940
happening right now.

00:21:56.440 --> 00:21:58.480
The Infrastructure Act, the CHIPS Act,

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:02.700
the IRA, all of these are offering an

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:05.180
ability for American manufacturing to

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:06.620
be able to compete with Chinese

